y John von Neumann at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey, who built one of the first computers to realize Alan Turing’s vision of a Universal Machine. Their work would break the distinction between numbers that mean things and numbers that do things—and our universe would never be the same. Using five kilobytes of memory (the amount allocated to displaying the cursor on a computer desktop of today), they achieved unprecedented success in both weather prediction and nuclear weapons design, while tackling, in their spare time, problems ranging from the evolution of viruses to the evolution of stars. Dyson’s account, both historic and prophetic, sheds important new light on how the digital universe exploded in the aftermath of World War II. The proliferation of both codes and machines was paralleled by two historic developments: the decoding of self-replicating sequences in biology and the invention of the hydrogen bomb. It’s no coincidence that the most destructive and the most constructive of human inventions appeared at exactly the same time. How did code take over the world?","pageCount":466,"publicationYear":2012,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Biography & Autobiography","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-turing-s-cathedral-the-origins-of-the-digital-universe-george-dyson"}],"displaySummary":"In Turing’s Cathedral, George Dyson focuses on a small group of men and women, led by John von Neumann at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey, who built on a computer desktop of today), they achieved unprecedented success in both weather prediction and nuclear weapons design, while tackling, in their spare time, problems ranging from the evolution of viruses to the evolution of stars. Dyson’s account, both historic and prophetic, sheds important new light on how the digital universe exploded in the aftermath of World War II. The proliferation of both codes and machines was paralleled by two historic developments: the decoding of self-replicating sequences in biology and the invention of the hydrogen bomb. It’s no coincidence that the most destructive and the most constructive of human inventions appeared at exactly the same time. How did code take over the world?","primaryTopic":"Technology, Computing & AI","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Technology","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Technology","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Technology","mappedSubject":"Technology","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily explores the technological advancements in computing and its implications, framed within a historical context of the people involved, notably Alan Turing and John von Neumann.","id":"subject-evidence-book-turing-s-cathedral-the-origins-of-the-digital-universe-george-dyson-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Biography & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":7,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-turing-s-cathedral-the-origins-of-the-digital-universe-george-dyson","id":"subject-evidence-book-turing-s-cathedral-the-origins-of-the-digital-universe-george-dyson-5"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Biography & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Biography","score":6,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-turing-s-cathedral-the-origins-of-the-digital-universe-george-dyson","id":"subject-evidence-book-turing-s-cathedral-the-origins-of-the-digital-universe-george-dyson-4"},{"source":"keyword_classifier","rawLabel":"Business & Economics","mappedSubject":"Business & Economics","score":2,"confidence":"low","note":"world history keyword; war/military keyword; science keyword; nature/environment keyword; technology keyword; business/economics keyword; gender/sexuality keyword","id":"subject-eviden